The provided code snippet is a part of the `LibraryManager` class in the `Emby.Server.Implementations.Library` namespace. The class has a method `LogError` which is called with an exception and a message. The method is not defined in the provided code snippet, but it is likely that it logs the error using a logging framework.

To write unit tests for this method, we can use a mocking framework like Moq to mock the dependencies of the `LibraryManager` class. We can then call the `LogError` method and verify that the expected logging behavior occurs.

Here is an example of how we can write a unit test for the `LogError` method:
```csharp
using Emby.Server.Implementations.Library;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Moq;
using Xunit;

namespace Emby.Server.Implementations.Library.Tests
{
    public class LibraryManagerTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public void LogError_CalledWithExceptionAndMessage_LoggerLogsError()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<LibraryManager>>();
            var libraryManager = new LibraryManager(
                Mock.Of<IServerApplicationHost>(),
                Mock.Of<ILoggerFactory>(),
                Mock.Of<ITaskManager>(),
                Mock.Of<IUserManager>(),
                Mock.Of<IServerConfigurationManager>(),
                Mock.Of<IUserDataManager>(),
                Mock.Of<Lazy<ILibraryMonitor>>(),
                Mock.Of<IFileSystem>(),
                Mock.Of<Lazy<IProviderManager>>(),
                Mock.Of<Lazy<IUserViewManager>>(),
                Mock.Of<IMediaEncoder>(),
                Mock.Of<IItemRepository>(),
                Mock.Of<IItemPersistenceService>(),
                Mock.Of<INextUpService>(),
                Mock.Of<IItemCountService>(),
                Mock.Of<ILinkedChildrenService>(),
                Mock.Of<IImageProcessor>(),
                Mock.Of<NamingOptions>(),
                Mock.Of<IDirectoryService>(),
                Mock.Of<IPeopleRepository>(),
                Mock.Of<IPathManager>(),
                Mock.Of<DotIgnoreIgnoreRule>()
            );
            libraryManager._logger = loggerMock.Object;

            var exception = new Exception("Test exception");
            var message = "Test message";

            // Act
            libraryManager.LogError(exception, message);

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogError(exception, message), Times.Once);
        }
    }
}
```
This test creates a mock `ILogger<LibraryManager>` object and sets it as the `_logger` field of the `LibraryManager` instance. It then calls the `LogError` method with an exception and a message, and verifies that the `LogError` method is called on the mock logger with the same exception and message.

Note that this test assumes that the `LogError` method is defined in the `LibraryManager` class and that it logs the error using the `_logger` field. If the `LogError` method is not defined in the `LibraryManager` class, or if it logs the error using a different mechanism, the test will need to be modified accordingly.
